          {
            "id": "element-14",
            "type": "text",
            "x": 469.65,
            "y": 138.16,
            "w": 754.76,
            "h": 96.4,
            "fill": "transparent",
            "stroke": "transparent",
            "strokeWidth": 0,
            "text": "Target has a foundation for secure development, but developer-specific training should be formalized to reduce knowledge gaps\na)     General security training is provided, and Target responded to audit feedback by holding a hands-on OWASP-style seminar for developers, creating a foundation for ongoing role-based secure coding education\nb)     Developer-specific secure coding training isn't yet recurring or consistently tracked; making it annual would help keep secure coding expectations current as frameworks, AI-assisted coding, and attack patterns evolve",
            "align": "left",
            "valign": "top",
            "fontSize": 11,
            "fontFace": "ヒラギノ角ゴ Pro W3",
            "bold": true,
            "textColor": "111827",
            "runs": [
              {
                "text": "Target has a foundation for secure development, but developer-specific training should be formalized to reduce knowledge gaps",
                "bold": true,
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "ヒラギノ角ゴ Pro W3",
                "fontSize": 11,
                "breakLine": true
              },
              {
                "text": "a)     General security training is provided, and Target responded to audit feedback by holding a hands-on OWASP-style seminar for developers, creating a foundation for ongoing role-based secure coding education",
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "Arial",
                "fontSize": 9,
                "breakLine": true
              },
              {
                "text": "b)     Developer-specific secure coding training isn't yet recurring or consistently tracked; making it annual would help keep secure coding expectations current as frameworks, AI-assisted coding, and attack patterns evolve",
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "Arial",
                "fontSize": 9,
                "breakLine": true
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "element-15",
            "type": "text",
            "x": 469.65,
            "y": 237.42,
            "w": 754.76,
            "h": 81.86,
            "fill": "transparent",
            "stroke": "transparent",
            "strokeWidth": 0,
            "text": "Although secure design practices are in place through technical requirements and design reviews, they need to be expanded for higher-risk workflows to catch authorization, PHI, integration, and AI risks earlier\na)     Product and engineering teams turn business needs into technical requirements, designs, estimates, and timelines. However, formal threat modeling, abuse-case review, and security architecture checkpoints were not evidenced, creating an opportunity to add lightweight secure design reviews for authorization, PHI, integration, and AI risks before coding to improve security",
            "align": "left",
            "valign": "top",
            "fontSize": 11,
            "fontFace": "ヒラギノ角ゴ Pro W3",
            "bold": true,
            "textColor": "111827",
            "runs": [
              {
                "text": "Although secure design practices are in place through technical requirements and design reviews, they need to be expanded for higher-risk workflows to catch authorization, PHI, integration, and AI risks earlier",
                "bold": true,
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "ヒラギノ角ゴ Pro W3",
                "fontSize": 11,
                "breakLine": true
              },
              {
                "text": "a)     Product and engineering teams turn business needs into technical requirements, designs, estimates, and timelines. However, formal threat modeling, abuse-case review, and security architecture checkpoints were not evidenced, creating an opportunity to add lightweight secure design reviews for authorization, PHI, integration, and AI risks before coding to improve security",
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "Arial",
                "fontSize": 9,
                "breakLine": true
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "element-16",
            "type": "text",
            "x": 469.65,
            "y": 322.13,
            "w": 754.76,
            "h": 140.02,
            "fill": "transparent",
            "stroke": "transparent",
            "strokeWidth": 0,
            "text": "Security testing encompasses annual third-party penetration assessments; however, testing at the release level should be enhanced to catch security issues before deploying to production\na)     Target conducts annual third-party application penetration testing, including credentialed tests to validate role controls and data segregation, providing independent validation of key controls and a foundation for security checks\nb)     Security testing is not embedded in each major release through routine security regression testing, or automated pre-production gates; adding these controls would reduce the likelihood that vulnerabilities are found only during annual testing or after release\nc)     Snyk, a vulnerability scanning tool, was seen as too noisy and underused; tuning SCA settings, setting severity thresholds, and tracking exceptions would improve visibility into vulnerable libraries without overwhelming developers",
            "align": "left",
            "valign": "top",
            "fontSize": 11,
            "fontFace": "ヒラギノ角ゴ Pro W3",
            "bold": true,
            "textColor": "111827",
            "runs": [
              {
                "text": "Security testing encompasses annual third-party penetration assessments; however, testing at the release level should be enhanced to catch security issues before deploying to production",
                "bold": true,
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "ヒラギノ角ゴ Pro W3",
                "fontSize": 11,
                "breakLine": true
              },
              {
                "text": "a)     Target conducts annual third-party application penetration testing, including credentialed tests to validate role controls and data segregation, providing independent validation of key controls and a foundation for security checks",
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "Arial",
                "fontSize": 9,
                "breakLine": true
              },
              {
                "text": "b)     Security testing is not embedded in each major release through routine security regression testing, or automated pre-production gates; adding these controls would reduce the likelihood that vulnerabilities are found only during annual testing or after release",
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "Arial",
                "fontSize": 9,
                "breakLine": true
              },
              {
                "text": "c)     Snyk, a vulnerability scanning tool, was seen as too noisy and underused; tuning SCA settings, setting severity thresholds, and tracking exceptions would improve visibility into vulnerable libraries without overwhelming developers",
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "Arial",
                "fontSize": 9,
                "breakLine": true
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "element-19",
            "type": "text",
            "x": 469.65,
            "y": 465.01,
            "w": 754.76,
            "h": 96.4,
            "fill": "transparent",
            "stroke": "transparent",
            "strokeWidth": 0,
            "text": "Secure release practices involve robust GitHub and QA controls; however, AppSec criteria should also be incorporated into the release readiness process to reduce security release risk\na)     GitHub branch protections require pull requests and approvals, limit merge/deploy rights, and include QA review as part of the delivery flow, reducing the likelihood of unreviewed code reaching production and creating a foundation for adding security-specific release gates\nb)     Incorporating security scans, SCA findings, design reviews, and remediation checks into release criteria adds security quality gates, helping identify, assign ownership, and resolve high-risk issues before deployment",
            "align": "left",
            "valign": "top",
            "fontSize": 11,
            "fontFace": "ヒラギノ角ゴ Pro W3",
            "bold": true,
            "textColor": "111827",
            "runs": [
              {
                "text": "Secure release practices involve robust GitHub and QA controls; however, AppSec criteria should also be incorporated into the release readiness process to reduce security release risk",
                "bold": true,
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "ヒラギノ角ゴ Pro W3",
                "fontSize": 11,
                "breakLine": true
              },
              {
                "text": "a)     GitHub branch protections require pull requests and approvals, limit merge/deploy rights, and include QA review as part of the delivery flow, reducing the likelihood of unreviewed code reaching production and creating a foundation for adding security-specific release gates",
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "Arial",
                "fontSize": 9,
                "breakLine": true
              },
              {
                "text": "b)     Incorporating security scans, SCA findings, design reviews, and remediation checks into release criteria adds security quality gates, helping identify, assign ownership, and resolve high-risk issues before deployment",
                "color": "111827",
                "fontFace": "Arial",
                "fontSize": 9,
                "breakLine": true
              }
            ]
          },
          {
